	1793 [label="As you stated, we... may never know. And it may not be for us to know. Such is the way of things. But it does not change, I think, the years I spent in service to an organization that meant, at its heart, to do kind deeds in this world.",shape=box,color=blue];
		1793 -> 1753;
	1794 [label="Perhaps you are right. Perhaps our silver lady would only give such a tragic blessing. She would not wish to remove the memory of pain, such as with Roathe.",shape=box,color=blue];
		1794 -> 1754;
	1795 [label="But it does not change, I think, the years I spent in service to an organization that meant, at its heart, to do kind deeds in this world.",shape=box,color=blue];
		1795 -> 1753;
	1797 [label="Do you agree with Roathe and me about the Man in the Wall not being a real god?",shape=box,color=red];
		1797 -> 1800;
	1799 [label="Where do you think the Indifference falls in this?",shape=box,color=red];
		1799 -> 1801;
	1798 [label="Do you side with Roathe in thinking the Indifference is a real god or not?",shape=box,color=red];
		1798 -> 1800;
	1800 [label="I do not know where I stand on the matter. I haven't enough evidence to make a decision either way. But I suppose it does not change much, does it?",shape=box,color=blue];
		1800 -> 1802;
		1800 -> 1803;
	1801 [label="Whether or not the Indifference is a god, I do not know where I stand on the matter. I haven't enough evidence to make a decision either way. But I suppose it does not change much, does it?",shape=box,color=blue];
		1801 -> 1802;
		1801 -> 1803;
	1802 [label="I think it changes a great deal. If it isn't a real god - it's made by people. That means it can be defeated by people.",shape=box,color=red];
		1802 -> 1804;
	1803 [label="Cosa intendi?",shape=box,color=red];
		1803 -> 1805;
	1804 [label="If you had no hope that it could be defeated by our efforts, why bother stand against it in the first place? Truly, you are a baffling creature.",shape=box,color=blue];
		1804 -> 1806;
	1805 [label="The method of its arrival into our universe does not change the methods by which we might defeat it.",shape=box,color=blue];
		1805 -> 1807;
	1806 [label="You cannot possibly mean those words. Are you simply \"going along\" with the actions of others, then? Fighting it for lack of anything better to do with your time?",shape=box,color=blue];
		1806 -> 1808;
	1807 [label="Behold: a god of our creation, sewn from the fabric of our own emptiness.",shape=box,color=blue];
		1807 -> 1809;
	1808 [label="Surely you could think of a better hobby.",shape=box,color=blue];
		1808 -> 1810;
		1808 -> 1811;
	1809 [label="A fabric doll we might over-fill with sadness, our grief, our pain, our anger, our agony, until the stitches threaten to burst with it.",shape=box,color=blue];
		1809 -> 1812;
	1810 [label="No, I didn't think it was hopeless - that's not what I meant.",shape=box,color=red];
		1810 -> 1813;
	1811 [label="What I meant was, this changes how we can think about killing it. Understanding it.",shape=box,color=red];
		1811 -> 1814;
	1812 [label="You will pardon me if I find that fills me with far more dread than if it were a god before we arrived. ",shape=box,color=blue];
		1812 -> 1815;
	1813 [label="Thinking before one puts words into the world is not a prerequisite. Neither is it for bullets. ",shape=box,color=blue];
		1813 -> 1807;
	1814 [label="I do not believe that is true. I would argue that we understand just as little about its motivations now as we might if it were some \"great and unknowable god.\"",shape=box,color=blue];
		1814 -> 1807;
	1815 [label="Yes, in fact, I think I prefer that.",shape=box,color=blue];
		1815 -> 1816;
	1816 [label="There might be some hope that it could be more intelligent than humanity at large.",shape=box,color=blue];
		1816 -> 1817;
	1817 [label="With that... utterly bleak thought, I think I shall leave you for the day.",shape=box,color=blue];
		1817 -> 1818;
